OK, I've done a little flow analysis.

Unless I'm totally misreading things:
  The player is always the last monster in the queue for updates.
  New monsters are generated at a point where nobody is free to act.
  New monsters are generated *after* everyone receives energy for the
  next round.
  Nethack's speed system looks horribly like a bodge.

As far as I can tell, you *should* always get one turn before any
freshly generated monster gets to act, unless you are burdened or
moving at a less-than-normal speed for some other reason.

Infuriatingly (well, it mildly infuriates *me*, anyway), being fast does
not give you a straightforward 33.3333% increase in your speed.  Instead,
it gives you a 50% increase in speed 2/3 of the time.  This does have
some interesting tactical effects (in particular, it means you can't go
"move-move-hit-move" and take no harm from a normal speed monster.), but
it also leads to some, um, mildly counterintuitive effects when it
interacts with the code for being burdened.

Being burdened is a strict 25% penalty to your speed after the
calculations for being fast or very fast are made.  A very fast
character will never lose a turn (relative to a normal-speed monster)
for being Burdened, but a character who is merely fast will lose a turn
every third time (on average) that the movement calculation code runs.

Now, for that hit from the soldier ant.
The soldier ant's first attack does 2d4 damage, for a maximum of 8; this
has no special effects.
Its second attack does 3d4 damage, for a maximum of 12; it is also a
strength poison.  This means it has a 1 in 8 chance, if cancellation
does not apply, of poisoning you; attribute poisons have a poison rating
of 30.

A poisoned attack, not from a thrown weapon, has a 1-in-rating chance of
killing you stone dead on the spot (thrown weapons have a hidden +20 to
their poison rating) unless it is a Charisma poison.  Otherwise, it has
a 5-in-rating chance of draining the relevant stat by 3-5 points.
Failing this, the poison attack does 10-15 points of extra damage
(unless it's a poisoned weapon, in which case it does 1-6 points.)

35 hit points is the theoretical limit, and highly unlikely for a wizard
(since wizards have MC3 cloaks normally, giving them a 98% chance of
evading a monster's special close combat attack effects).  A wizard,
therefore, probably takes 20 points of damage max (and that's pretty
unlikely).  Hence, you were clearly injured, since even an elven 8th
level wizard is really quite unlikely to only have 20hp (since this
requires you to have gained no more than 9hp in seven level gains; I
don't think I've ever done *that* badly on my hit points, and it's
impossible if your Con has always been 15 or higher).

Had you been in good health, you would almost certainly have survived
long enough to take action.

Had you been possessed of a negative armour class, damage reduction due
to negative AC would probably have saved you (and you probably wouldn't
have taken both hits, either).

Had you been of a lower experience level, you might not have had the
soldier ant generated.

I fail to see a problem; the death you describe is *staggeringly*
unlikely, especially compared to an ordinary "oh shit. I just walked
round a corner and there's a soldier ant in front of me." death.

